  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 316 PM EDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nThe upper level flow becomes more zonal Sunday into Monday, with a \nweakening of the western US ridge and weakly rising heights over the \nOhio Valley. This period continues to look relatively dry and hot, \nwith highs in the lower 90s. LREF members point to a very small \nchance (10-20%) for an isolated pulse shower/storm Sunday afternoon, \nbut confidence in development is low. Heat index values could \napproach triple digits each afternoon, especially over the western \nhalf of the area. \n\nTuesday's a bit of a transition day. We start to see a more active \nNW flow pattern take shape. We could end up staying dry through most \nof Tuesday, but we also cannot rule out isolated to scattered \nconvection in the afternoon and evening hours. A larger thunderstorm \ncomplex could take aim at our area as early as late Tuesday. \n\nMultiple bouts of convection then look possible Wednesday through \nFriday of next week. Strong storms and heavy rainfall will be \npossible at times, which could result in some instances of excessive \nrainfall and flash flooding. Much too early to try to get into the \ndetails with these storms, however. Tuesday should be one more day \ninto the lower 90s, but temperatures will fall back through the 80s \nfor highs during the second half of the week due to increased cloud \ncover/precipitation.",
